data.reasoningText:
Now I have a good understanding of the project. Let me plan and implement.

## Component Plan: Team Messaging Chat

### Component Tree
```
Chat.razor                    (page — owns all state, orchestrates layout)
├── ChannelSidebar.razor      (channel list, active selection)
│   └── ChannelItem.razor     (single channel row)
├── MessageArea.razor         (main message panel)
│   ├── MessageBubble.razor   (single message with reactions, actions)
│   └── MessageInput.razor    (textarea + send button, reused)
└── ThreadPanel.razor         (collapsible thread view)
```

Now I'm thinking through the implementation strategy. I'll start with the service contracts and models, then build the components from the bottom up. For organization, I'll create a dedicated `Components/Pages/Chat/` folder to keep all chat-related components together, with the main `Chat.razor` page at the root of Pages.

I need to set up the folder structure with individual component files for the sidebar, channel items, message bubbles, input area, and thread panel. I'll also need to add the appropriate `@using` directives to `_Imports.razor` so the Chat subfolder components are accessible, and create a `Services/` folder at the root level for the service contracts and models.

```

csharp
// Services/IChannelService.cs
public interface IChannelService
{
    Task<List<Channel>> GetChannelsAsync();
    Task<Channel> GetChannelAsync(string id);
}

// Services/IMessageService.cs
public interface IMessageService
{
    Task<List<Message>> GetMessagesAsync(string channelId, int skip, int take);
    Task<Message> SendMessageAsync(string channelId, string content);
    Task<List<Message>> GetThreadMessagesAsync(string rootMessageId, int skip, int take);
    Task<Message> ReplyToThreadAsync(string rootMessageId, string content);
}

// Services/Models.cs
public class Channel
{
    public string Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Description { get; set; }
}

public class Message
{
    public string Id { get; set; }
    public string Content { get; set; }
    public string AuthorId { get; set; }
    public string AuthorName { get; set; }
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; set; }
    public List<Reaction> Reactions { get; set; }
    public int ReplyCount { get; set; }
}

public class Reaction
{
    public string Emoji { get; set; }
    public int Count { get; set; }
}
```

Now I'm thinking through the data flow. The Chat page component will manage the state for channels, selected channel, messages, thread state, and pagination. It'll pass channels and selection callbacks to the sidebar, messages and thread callbacks to the message area, and thread details to the thread panel. I'll also need a helper method for formatting relative timestamps.

For styling, each component gets its own scoped CSS file. Let me start building the components from the bottom up, beginning with the service contracts and models, then the leaf components like ChannelItem and MessageBubble, working up to the container components.

For the MessageArea pagination, I'm considering using scroll-to-top detection with JavaScript interop to load older messages, but I'll keep it simple for now. I'll use a collocated JavaScript module for the MessageArea component to handle scroll detection with an IntersectionObserver on a sentinel element at the top of the message list. When that sentinel becomes visible, I'll trigger a .NET callback to load older messages. For the emoji picker, I'm planning a small popup with a handful of common emojis that appears when clicking the "React" button. Now I'm drafting the Razor components...

Writing component markup... Writing component markup... Finishing the reaction display and adding the hover action buttons for replying and reacting with emoji... Finishing the component logic with the emoji selection handler and a utility function to format timestamps in a human-readable way.

For the MessageArea component, I'm composing the message bubble and input together with scroll-based pagination. I'll use JavaScript interop with a collocated module to handle scroll-to-top detection via an IntersectionObserver, which will trigger loading more messages when the user scrolls near the top.

Writing scroll observer... Writing component markup... Now I'm planning the implementation strategy—I'll need to create the service interface first, set up the component directory structure, then build out all the files in sequence. For the timestamp formatting, I should use DateTime.UtcNow consistently with the message timestamps to avoid timezone mismatches. When the Chat component initializes, it'll need to load the available channels, select the first one by default, and then fetch that channel's messages to populate the view. newlines. Let me think about the best approach here - I could handle this with JavaScript interop to intercept the Enter key and conditionally prevent the default behavior, or I could simplify it by just accepting that Enter adds a newline and requiring users to click the Send button, which is actually a common pattern in chat applications. I'm going to skip the Enter-to-send feature for now and keep things straightforward. Now I need to start writing out all the code files—I'll batch create the directories and files together. But first, I should think through how to handle thread summaries since the IChatService has GetThreadAsync but I need to figure out how to populate ThreadSummary records from the message data.

Looking at the API, messages have ReplyToId fields, so I can identify which messages are thread starters by checking if other messages reference them. The practical approach is to show a "replies" link for messages that have threads and track which ones have replies by examining the loaded messages. I'll track thread summaries in the Chat page state as threads are opened and loaded, building up the reply counts dynamically rather than trying to fetch them all upfront. keep it straightforward: every message gets a "Reply" button that opens the thread, and I'll track loaded thread summaries in a dictionary so I can display reply counts for messages I know have threads. When a thread loads, I'll update that summary. For the initial page load, I need to load channels, select the first one, fetch its messages with pagination, and mark it as read. I'll handle scrolling to load older messages by tracking the skip value and prepending them to the list. Now I'm ready to start writing the implementation, beginning with the JavaScript interop for scroll detection. I'll leverage Bootstrap's existing classes and add CSS isolation for custom styling where needed. Now I'm mapping out the file structure—I need to create the service interface, then build out all the chat components with their corresponding stylesheets and the JavaScript interop module for scroll detection.

I should organize everything under a dedicated `Components/Chat/` folder and make sure the namespaces are properly configured in `_Imports.razor` so all the components and services are accessible throughout the project.

Now I'm defining the service interfaces and data models—setting up records for channels, messages, reactions, and thread summaries, then creating the IChatService interface with methods to fetch and manage chat data.

Building the channel item component... Writing component styles... Writing component markup... Writing component styles... Adding button and component styles... Finishing the message actions with reply and emoji reaction buttons, then adding an emoji picker dropdown that displays common emojis to select from, and finally setting up the component parameters including the Message and optional ThreadSummary properties. Finishing the emoji selection by invoking the callback, then adding a helper method that formats timestamps into relative time strings like "just now" or "5 min ago", and starting to style the message bubble component.

Writing component styles... Still writing styles... Still writing styles...

Now I'm moving to the MessageArea component which needs JavaScript interop to handle scroll detection for loading older messages. Adding component parameters... Writing component code... Adding more styles... Writing component markup... Adding more styles... Still writing styles... Finishing the MessageArea component with thread and reaction handlers, then setting up the code section with the channel list, selected channel state, and message collection. Writing component methods... Adding more methods... Writing component styles...

Now I need to finalize the imports in the _Imports.razor file to include the Services namespace so the components can access the Message, Reaction, and ThreadSummary types, and add the Chat components namespace so the page can reference its sub-components. Then I'll create the directory structure and generate all the files together. I realize EventCallback handles the StateHasChanged automatically after invoking the callback, so I can simplify by removing the explicit call and just merging the logic into a single CloseThread method that clears the thread state.

